\subsection{Smart Compress: Learned Compression}
\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}

To test whether learned compression can bypass the cliff-edge, we insert a cross-attention pooling module between SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector and LLM backbone. The module consists of $M$ learned query vectors that attend over all 128 source tokens through multi-head cross-attention (12 heads, 960 dimensions), producing $M$ compressed representations. The compressor adds 22.2M parameters ($\sim$5\% of SmolVLA) and is trained on the full LIBERO dataset (40 tasks across four suites) for 100K steps with the vision encoder frozen and the LLM backbone unfrozen (116.8M trainable parameters total, 25.9\% of the model). We evaluate $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$, where $M = 128$ is a passthrough control. To verify that the benefit stems from re-encoding rather than a specific attention mechanism, we train a parameter-matched MLP adapter (3-layer MLP with expansion ratio 4, 22.14M parameters, average pooling to $M$ tokens) under identical conditions. Training details are in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}.

tool_args.new_string:
\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}
\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}

We insert a cross-attention pooling module (22.2M parameters, $\sim$5\% of SmolVLA) between the pixel-shuffle connector and LLM backbone. $M$ learned queries attend over all 128 source tokens, producing $M$ compressed representations. We evaluate $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$ ($M{=}128$ is a passthrough control). A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) serves as an architecture control. Training details are in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}.

